deep.reconstruction:
Setting: X is a smooth projective variety of dimension n, H*(X) is a Weil cohomology, and ξ (elsewhere L) is cup product with a hyperplane class. Transposition is taken with respect to Poincaré duality.

LEMMA (abstract, 'algebra of operators' form). Let 𝒜 be a ring of endomorphisms of the abelian group H*(X) such that:
- 𝒜 is stable under taking homogeneous components;
- 𝒜 is stable under transposition;
- ξ ∈ 𝒜;
- for every 0 ≤ i < n there is u_i ∈ 𝒜 of degree −2(n−i) with u_i ξ^{n−i} = id on H^i(X), i.e. the composite H^i → H^{2n−i} → H^i is the identity.

Then 𝒜 contains:
- Λ, the adjoint/inverse Lefschetz operator;
- the projections onto all summands ξ^j P^i (i ≤ n, j ≤ n−i) of the Lefschetz primitive decomposition, where P^i ⊂ H^i is the primitive part.

In the intended application 𝒜 is the ring of algebraic correspondences acting on H*(X). This ring is stable under transposition (transposing cycles on X×X), and the marginal note '(et algébrique)' signals that the u_i should be algebraic. Stability under homogeneous components is precisely what is not automatic: it is the Künneth issue, condition C.

COROLLARY. The following are equivalent:
- (i) Λ is algebraic;
- (ii) for each 1 ≤ i ≤ n there is an algebraic correspondence inducing H^{n+i} → H^{n−i} inverse to ξ^i. (The written 'H^{2n−i} → H^i inverse of ξ^i' is an index slip, see errors.)

If these hold, the following are also algebraic:
- the projections onto the factors of the primitive decomposition;
- the further projections listed (the qualifier is illegible);
- the Künneth projectors H* → H^i.

In other words, B(X) implies C(X) and gives an algebraic primitive decomposition.

PROOF (begun). Under transposition the projection onto ξ^j P^i goes to the projection onto ξ^{n−i−j} P^i, since the Lefschetz decomposition is orthogonal for the Poincaré-type pairing. So it suffices to treat i + 2j ≤ n. Grothendieck then starts an induction on k = i + 2j, trivial for k < 0. The page breaks off here.

Presumably the induction peels off ξ^j P^i from H^k. One uses u_k ξ^{n−k} and its transposes to build the projector onto ξ H^{k−2} inside H^k, which is a polynomial in ξ and the u's. One then subtracts the already-constructed projectors.

Purpose: this is the formal mechanism behind the statements in the Bombay paper 'Standard conjectures on algebraic cycles': B(X) (Lefschetz type) is equivalent to the algebraicity of the inverse of L^{n−i}, and implies C(X) (Künneth). The previous page, an English letter, deals with weak Lefschetz variants A', A'' and their equivalence with B.
